{% set local_problem %}
<p>Sand Mountain's soil creates situations where surface drainage just isn't enough. We worked on a house near Ridge Road last year where the homeowner had been fighting wet basement issues for a decade. He'd tried everything - longer downspout extensions, French drains along the foundation, even a sump pump. Nothing worked for long.</p>

<p>The problem was the lot. His house sat at the low point of a gentle slope, and water from 3 downspouts plus some sheet flow from the yard all ended up pooling near his foundation. The extensions moved water 6 feet away - but on that slope, "6 feet away" just meant "6 feet before it runs right back."</p>

<p>We ran 4-inch underground drains from each downspout to a discharge point 45 feet away at the property line, plus a catch basin where the sheet flow collected. Total project was about $2,800. That was 14 months ago. Zero basement water since.</p>

<p>Not every property needs underground drains. But for the ones that do, it's the only real solution. Surface fixes just relocate the problem temporarily.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sloped channel to a discharge point far from your foundation.</p>

<p>The basic setup: downspout adapter at the top connects to 4-inch solid PVC or corrugated pipe buried 10-12 inches deep. The pipe runs at a 1% slope minimum (1 inch drop per 8 feet) to a discharge point - either daylight exit at property edge, pop-up emitter in the lawn, or dry well that disperses water into the soil.</p>

<p>We use Schedule 35 PVC for most runs - it's rigid, smooth inside for good flow, and handles lawn mower traffic better than corrugated. For longer runs or situations with lots of turns, we might use corrugated because it's more flexible.</p>

<p>Pop-up emitters work well for most Albertville installs. They stay flush with the lawn until water flows, then pop open to release. When flow stops, they close to keep debris and critters out. The alternative - daylight exits - are simpler but only work if you have a lower point at the property edge.</p>

<p>Check out some of our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent underground drain installations</a> in Marshall County.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
